# Thermo Control — 240L Aquarium

Water temperature control system for a 240L aquarium, built on a Waveshare
ESP32-S3-Pico. ON/OFF hysteresis (heater via SSR, fan via PWM), alarms (buzzer +
event log), energy usage measurement/estimation, web dashboard, captive-portal
provisioning. Persistent data (config, history, alarms) lives in SPI FRAM
(MB85RS64V, 8KB).

Design rationale lives in `docs/` (see table below). This file only covers what's
needed to build/upload/operate the firmware.

## Hardware

| Component        | Value                                                              |
|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| MCU               | Waveshare ESP32-S3-Pico (ESP32-S3, dual-core, WiFi)                 |
| Temp sensor       | STS35 (Sensirion), I2C1 (Wire1), address 0x4B                       |
| RTC               | DS3231, I2C0 (Wire), NTP-synced                                     |
| FRAM              | MB85RS64V, SPI, 8KB (0x0000–0x1FFF)                                  |
| Heater            | SSR (active LOW)                                                     |
| Fan               | MOSFET PWM (Iduino ST1168, LEDC)                                     |
| Energy meter      | GPIO pulse input (ISR, no boot glitch)                               |
| Buzzer            | passive, HIGH = ON                                                   |
| Flash             | 16MB (real chip — PlatformIO board profile `seeed_xiao_esp32s3` assumes 8MB + PSRAM neither of which match this board; see partition note below) |

**Partitions:** `board_build.partitions = app3M_fat9M_16MB.csv` (two 3MB OTA
slots, sized for the real 16MB flash — not the board profile's default). A
mismatched single-slot table here previously caused a hard crash on OTA
upload — keep two OTA slots when changing this.

### Pins (`src/hardware/hardware_pins.h` — source of truth)

| GPIO | Function | Notes |
|------|---------|-------|
| 1  | `BUZZER_PIN` | HIGH = ON |
| 2  | `FAN_PWM_PIN` | LEDC PWM |
| 5/6 | `I2C_SDA_PIN`/`I2C_SCL_PIN` | I2C0 — DS3231, cable <5cm |
| 7/8 | `STS35_SDA_PIN`/`STS35_SCL_PIN` | I2C1 — STS35, ~1m cable |
| 9  | `ENERGY_PULSE_PIN` | INPUT_PULLUP, ISR FALLING |
| 10–13 | `FRAM_SPI_*` | CS/MOSI/SCK/MISO, default IO_MUX SPI2/FSPI |
| 17 | `HEATER_SSR_PIN` | active LOW |
| 18 | `RESERVE_PIN` | INPUT_PULLUP — spare, physically adjacent to pin 17 |
| 37 | `RESET_PIN` | INPUT_PULLUP, hold 5s → provisioning |

Full board diagram: `docs/ESP32-S3-Pico.png`.

## Algorithm

Hysteresis around `target_temp` (25.0°C default):

```
Heater:  ON below target - heat_hyst (0.3°C),   OFF at target_temp

Fan uses a day/night thermal buffer (fan_on_point = target_temp + thermal_buffer,
1.0°C default) instead of target_temp directly — lets daytime heat surplus
accumulate instead of cooling it away immediately, so the heater works less at
night:
Fan:     ON above fan_on_point,                 100% above fan_on_point + cool_full (1.0°C)
         OFF at fan_on_point - cool_start (0.3°C), floor fan_min_pct (30%) in between
```

Validated: `thermal_buffer >= cool_start` (else OFF point dips below `target_temp`
and fights the heater), `cool_full < alarm_delta_high` (else ALARM_HIGH could fire
mid-ramp before the fan reaches 100%).

Alarms (see `src/algorithm/temp_algorithm.cpp`). Rule: an alarm that needs intervention is latched and
cannot be muted — the buzzer stops only after the fix and the reset; self-clearing temperature alarms can be
muted for the current episode (the cause may be out of reach, e.g. a heat wave).
- **TEMP_LOW** — temp below `target - alarm_delta_low` (2.0°C default), buzzer, self-heals once back in range; Mute
- **TEMP_HIGH** — temp above `fan_on_point + alarm_delta_high` (1.5°C default, measured from fan_on_point not target_temp), buzzer, self-heals once back in range; Mute
- **TREND** — warning only (no buzzer, yellow in the GUI, yellow dot on the charts): heater ON continuously ≥
  `trend_window_min` (60 min) without a `trend_alarm_delta` (0.2°C) rise — suspected heater power / configuration.
  Lasts while the heater runs without the rise; the tank itself is protected by TEMP_LOW / TEMP_HIGH
- **SENSOR_FAULT** — STS35 invalid past a threshold count — critical latch (heater/fan emergency-off), buzzer not silenced by Mute or Service Mode, needs a manual reset (`/api/reset-sensor-fault`)
- **HARDWARE** — FRAM, DS3231 RTC or Wi-Fi credentials missing from FRAM (build-time fallback in use), checked
  at boot, every 60 s and after a failed transfer (`src/hardware/hw_health.*`). Latched critical alarm: continuous
  buzzer (not silenced by Mute or Service Mode), red banner in the GUI, `hw_alarm*` in `/api/status`,
  `alarm` / `alarm_text` in `/api/health`. "Acknowledge" (`/api/hw-alarm-ack`) clears only the blocks that work
  again; the latch survives software restarts. Temperature control keeps running (it needs only the STS35).
  FRAM missing at boot → restart once it answers (max 3 per latch). RTC is retried at runtime and set from NTP.

Thresholds are configurable via `/api/thermo-config` (GUI). Full rationale for
choosing ON/OFF+SSR over PID: `docs/ALGORITHM_DECISION.md`.

## Build and upload

Requires [PlatformIO](https://platformio.org/).

```bash
# USB (default environment)
pio run -t upload
pio device monitor          # 115200 baud

# OTA (WiFi) — only works once the device is already running firmware with this code, see below
pio run -e seeed_xiao_esp32s3_ota -t upload --upload-port 192.168.10.4

# Remote logs (no USB) — requires firmware with the log-socket, see below
pio device monitor --port socket://<device-ip>:8880
```

**The first upload of firmware containing OTA/log-socket must go over USB** — neither
mechanism works until firmware that includes it is already running
(`ArduinoOTA`/`WiFiServer` don't exist in older builds). Subsequent uploads can go
through `-e seeed_xiao_esp32s3_ota`. Verified working end-to-end on hardware
(2026-07-24). Gotchas: `default_envs` keeps a bare `pio run` from building the OTA env;
the laptop and the device are in different subnets (MikroTik, isolated IoT VLAN), so
the return TCP connection needs a firewall rule — hence the fixed `--host_port=8266`;
the log socket accepts one client at a time.

**`OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_4` setup:** the shell variable is named after this
device's local IP (dots → underscores), not the project name — several IoT
projects share one `~/.secrets/iot.env`/`~/.zshrc`, and a name collision
(everyone using plain `OTA_PASSWORD`) risks flashing the wrong password to the
wrong device. `platformio.ini` reads it via `${sysenv.OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_4}`
into `build_flags` as a global `-D` — which also means PlatformIO/SCons
invalidates the build signature for *every* source file whenever the flag's
value changes between invocations. An unset/ad-hoc `export` in a fresh shell
causes a full from-scratch rebuild on every OTA upload, not just an
incremental one. Fix: set it once, permanently, outside the repo —
`~/.secrets/iot.env` (`chmod 600`, `export OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_4='...'`,
one line per IoT device) sourced from `~/.zshrc`:
```bash
[ -f ~/.secrets/iot.env ] && source ~/.secrets/iot.env
```
With a stable value, `pio run -e seeed_xiao_esp32s3_ota -t upload` goes back
to normal incremental builds. Quote the password in single quotes if it
contains shell-special characters (`#`, `$`, spaces).

## Provisioning (first-time setup)

1. Hold `RESET_PIN` (GPIO37) for 5+ seconds during boot.
2. Connect to AP `ESP32-WATER-SETUP` (password `setup12345`) — captive portal at `192.168.4.1`.
   (Note: the boot log in `main.cpp` prints a different string, "ESP32-THERMO-SETUP" —
   that's a stale log message, not the real SSID; still needs fixing.)
3. Enter WiFi credentials and an admin password.
4. Reboot → firmware loads credentials from FRAM (AES-256), connects to the configured network.

Fallback Wi-Fi (only without credentials in FRAM) is not in the repo: `scripts/wifi_secret.py` reads
`IOT_WIFI_FALLBACK_SSID` / `IOT_WIFI_FALLBACK_PASSWORD` from the environment (`~/.secrets/iot.env`) into a
generated header at build time. Not set = no fallback. Running on the fallback raises the HARDWARE alarm.

## Touch panel (ESP-NOW)

Status broadcast to the touch panel (`home_display-iot`) every 5 s and right after a change
(`src/network/espnow_panel.*`, wire format `src/network/thermo_proto.h` — the panel keeps a copy):
temperature, state, heater / fan, alarms and muted alarms, Service Mode countdown. Own frame magic and a key
derived from the Wi-Fi password (HMAC-SHA256/16), `WiFi.setSleep(false)`. Commands from the panel: next stage.
Counters and `fw_version` in `/api/status`.

## API (session-authenticated except `/api/health`)

| Endpoint | Method | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `/api/login`, `/api/logout` | POST | Session (cookie) |
| `/api/health` | GET | No auth |
| `/api/status` | GET | Full system status |
| `/api/history-hourly`, `/api/history-daily` | GET | Temperature-average ring buffers |
| `/api/alarm-events` | GET | Alarm event log |
| `/api/thermo-config` | GET/POST | Algorithm thresholds |
| `/api/mute-alarm` | POST | Silence active TEMP_LOW / TEMP_HIGH (current episode) |
| `/api/reset-sensor-fault` | POST | Clear the SENSOR_FAULT latch |
| `/api/hw-alarm-ack` | POST | Acknowledge the HARDWARE alarm (clears what works again) |
| `/api/system-toggle` | POST | Auto Mode / Service Mode |
| `/api/energy`, `/api/energy-reset-day`, `/api/energy-reset-total` | GET/POST | Energy counter |
| `/api/test/fan`, `/api/test/heater` | POST | Manual override (test) |
| `/api/verify-pin` | POST | Lock-PIN gate for settings edits |

## FRAM layout (8KB, `src/hardware/fram_constants.h`)

```
0x0000–0x0017   Magic ("THMO") + version
0x0018–0x0417   AES-256 encrypted credentials (WiFi, admin password hash, device ID)
0x0500–0x053F   ThermoConfig (48B) + checksum, fixed 64B slot (~28% headroom for future fields)
0x0540–0x054B   EnergyStore
0x054C–0x055D   Ring-buffer metadata (hourly/daily/alarm — count/wptr/checksum)
0x055E–0x05FF   free (~63% of this block, before the ring buffers below)
0x0600–0x06BF   Hourly ring (24 records × 8B)
0x06C0–0x122F   Daily ring (366 records × 8B)
0x1230–...      Alarm-event ring
0x1B90–0x1B9B   LockPin
0x1BA0–0x1CA1   free (was C-09 deviation state/reasons, removed 2026-07-24)
0x1CA2–0x1CA9   EnergyResetInfo
0x1FFF          Health-check scratch byte (hw_health)
```

## Source structure

```
src/
  main.cpp                    Entry point, setup()/loop(), OTA + log-socket
  algorithm/
    temp_algorithm.*           Heater/fan hysteresis, alarms, ring-buffer logging
    algorithm_config.h          FRAM structs, DEFAULT_*, ALARM_FLAG_*
  config/                      Settings, system enable/disable, credential loading
  core/
    logging.*                  LOG_INFO/WARNING/ERROR, log-socket (WiFiServer :8880)
  crypto/                      AES-256 + SHA-256 for credentials stored in FRAM
  hardware/
    fram_controller.*, fram_constants.h   FRAM SPI, layout, ring buffers
    hardware_pins.h             GPIO map (source of truth)
    heater_controller.*, fan_controller.*, temp_sensor.*, buzzer_controller.*
    rtc_controller.*, energy_meter.*
  network/                     WiFi manager
  provisioning/                Captive portal (AP, web server, button detection)
  security/                    Auth, sessions, rate limiting
  web/                         Web server, API handlers, dashboard HTML
```

